1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,932
In 1925, a man sat in a Paris hotel suite and sold the Eiffel Tower.

2
00:00:07,032 --> 00:00:08,793
Not a postcard.

3
00:00:08,893 --> 00:00:10,269
Not a model.

4
00:00:10,369 --> 00:00:17,196
The tower itself, three hundred meters of iron, to a man who handed over a briefcase of cash.

5
00:00:17,296 --> 00:00:22,019
And the part nobody believes: the buyer never went to the police.

6
00:00:22,119 --> 00:00:24,571
Because the con wasn't the lie.

7
00:00:24,671 --> 00:00:28,537
The con was what the lie let the victim do to himself.

8
00:00:28,637 --> 00:00:32,777
Stay with me, because this isn't even his best story.

9
00:00:32,877 --> 00:00:35,903
His best story involves Al Capone.

10
00:00:36,003 --> 00:00:42,825
Victor Lustig was born in 1890 in a small town in what is now the Czech Republic.

11
00:00:42,925 --> 00:00:52,940
By his twenties he spoke several languages, and he had a talent that looks harmless on paper: people trusted him within about ninety seconds.

12
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:55,915
He wasn't a thief in the ordinary sense.

13
00:00:56,015 --> 00:00:58,048
He didn't pick locks.

14
00:00:58,148 --> 00:01:00,427
He didn't break in anywhere.

15
00:01:00,527 --> 00:01:02,872
He waited to be invited.

16
00:01:02,972 --> 00:01:10,704
After the First World War, he worked the transatlantic liners, targeting rich travelers with time to kill.

17
00:01:10,804 --> 00:01:18,009
He had polished suits, a quiet manner, and a rule he'd later write down himself: never look bored.

18
00:01:18,109 --> 00:01:23,609
Paris, 1925, is where all of it comes together.

19
00:01:23,709 --> 00:01:25,729
Here's the situation.

20
00:01:25,829 --> 00:01:33,357
The Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World's Fair, and it was never meant to last.

21
00:01:33,457 --> 00:01:36,195
Its permit was for twenty years.

22
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:43,226
By 1925 it was expensive, rusting, and in need of constant repainting.

23
00:01:43,326 --> 00:01:47,980
Lustig reads a newspaper article about the cost of maintaining it.

24
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:50,017
And an idea forms.

25
00:01:50,117 --> 00:01:54,056
What if the government quietly decided to get rid of it?

26
00:01:54,156 --> 00:01:57,353
He forges official government stationery.

27
00:01:57,453 --> 00:02:07,461
He writes to six scrap-metal dealers, inviting them to a confidential meeting at the Hôtel de Crillon, one of the most luxurious hotels in Paris.

28
00:02:07,561 --> 00:02:15,149
At the meeting, he introduces himself as a deputy director-general of the Ministry of Posts and Telegraphs.

29
00:02:15,249 --> 00:02:17,595
And he tells them the story.

30
00:02:17,695 --> 00:02:20,382
The tower is a financial disaster.

31
00:02:20,482 --> 00:02:22,894
The government wants it gone.

32
00:02:22,994 --> 00:02:29,391
But if the public learns the plan, there will be outrage, so it has to be handled quietly.

33
00:02:29,491 --> 00:02:30,993
Sealed bids.

34
00:02:31,093 --> 00:02:32,890
Total secrecy.

35
00:02:32,990 --> 00:02:37,976
Six men leave that room thinking they've been handed the deal of the century.

36
00:02:38,076 --> 00:02:41,053
Only one of them will make Lustig rich.

37
00:02:41,153 --> 00:02:43,375
His name is André Poisson.

38
00:02:43,475 --> 00:02:47,257
Lustig spent a few days studying the six dealers.

39
00:02:47,357 --> 00:02:49,171
Poisson stood out.

40
00:02:49,271 --> 00:02:55,653
He was successful, but he was new to Paris's inner business circles, and he wanted in.

41
00:02:55,753 --> 00:02:57,737
His wife had doubts.

42
00:02:57,837 --> 00:03:01,758
Poisson did not want to be the man who hesitated.

43
00:03:01,858 --> 00:03:04,467
Lustig took Poisson on a drive.

44
00:03:04,567 --> 00:03:08,185
He hinted that civil servants aren't paid well.

45
00:03:08,285 --> 00:03:10,879
That his position was difficult.

46
00:03:10,979 --> 00:03:17,089
That a man in his situation might expect a little something to keep the paperwork moving.

47
00:03:17,189 --> 00:03:23,022
And Poisson, eager to prove he understood how the world worked, offered him a bribe.

48
00:03:23,122 --> 00:03:24,985
That's the whole trick.

49
00:03:25,085 --> 00:03:27,379
Lustig never asked for it.

50
00:03:27,479 --> 00:03:31,353
He let Poisson buy the tower AND bribe the official.

51
00:03:31,453 --> 00:03:33,640
Poisson paid for both.

52
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:39,091
A few days later, Lustig was on a train to Vienna with a case full of cash.

53
00:03:39,191 --> 00:03:41,986
Now, he waited for the headline.

54
00:03:42,086 --> 00:03:43,430
A scandal.

55
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:44,704
Police.

56
00:03:44,804 --> 00:03:46,393
An arrest warrant.

57
00:03:46,493 --> 00:03:47,681
Nothing.

58
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:54,066
Poisson was a man who had just bribed a government official and bought a national monument.

59
00:03:54,166 --> 00:04:01,460
Going to the police meant admitting he'd been a fool, and admitting he'd tried to buy his way into a crooked deal.

60
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:03,404
He stayed silent.

61
00:04:03,504 --> 00:04:06,641
Lustig read the Paris papers for weeks.

62
00:04:06,741 --> 00:04:07,929
Nothing.

63
00:04:08,029 --> 00:04:13,491
So he went back to Paris and did it all again with a new set of six dealers.

64
00:04:13,591 --> 00:04:16,591
This time one of them went to the police.

65
00:04:16,691 --> 00:04:21,580
Lustig escaped to the United States before anyone could catch him.

66
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,426
In America he took the idea further.

67
00:04:24,526 --> 00:04:30,392
He built a small wooden box with rollers and dials and called it the Romanian Box.

68
00:04:30,492 --> 00:04:36,790
Insert a real $100 bill, wait six hours, and a perfect copy emerges.

69
00:04:36,890 --> 00:04:39,253
He'd demonstrate it twice.

70
00:04:39,353 --> 00:04:41,315
Two perfect bills.

71
00:04:41,415 --> 00:04:51,492
The mark would buy the box, often for thousands of dollars, and by the time they discovered it only copied if you loaded real notes in, Lustig was gone.

72
00:04:51,592 --> 00:04:53,926
He sold it more than once.

73
00:04:54,026 --> 00:04:58,043
The price reportedly reached tens of thousands of dollars.

74
00:04:58,143 --> 00:05:00,608
And then he met Al Capone.

75
00:05:00,708 --> 00:05:07,049
The story goes: Lustig asked Capone for a $50,000 investment in a scheme.

76
00:05:07,149 --> 00:05:10,242
He promised to double it in sixty days.

77
00:05:10,342 --> 00:05:18,302
Capone, who did not lend money to strangers, handed it over, with a warning of what would happen if it went wrong.

78
00:05:18,402 --> 00:05:22,886
Lustig put the cash in a safe-deposit box and did nothing.

79
00:05:22,986 --> 00:05:28,797
Two months later he returned to Capone, apologetic: the plan had failed.

80
00:05:28,897 --> 00:05:31,755
Here's your $50,000 back.

81
00:05:31,855 --> 00:05:37,191
Capone, so impressed by the honesty, handed him $5,000.

82
00:05:37,291 --> 00:05:40,119
Lustig had never planned to invest.

83
00:05:40,219 --> 00:05:45,700
He just wanted a loan he could hold for a few weeks, and he left with a gift.

84
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:49,165
His mistake was the one con men always make.

85
00:05:49,265 --> 00:05:50,752
He got greedy.

86
00:05:50,852 --> 00:05:56,413
By the 1930s he was running a counterfeiting operation in New York.

87
00:05:56,513 --> 00:05:59,731
In 1935 the U.S.

88
00:05:59,831 --> 00:06:02,370
Secret Service arrested him.

89
00:06:02,470 --> 00:06:12,059
Weeks later he escaped from federal detention by hanging a rope of bedsheets out the window, pretending to be a window washer in front of a crowd.

90
00:06:12,159 --> 00:06:16,266
It took the Secret Service about a month to find him.

91
00:06:16,366 --> 00:06:20,841
He was sentenced to 20 years and sent to Alcatraz.

92
00:06:20,941 --> 00:06:26,575
In 1947 he died of pneumonia in a federal prison hospital.

93
00:06:26,675 --> 00:06:32,710
On his death certificate, under occupation, it said: "salesman."

94
00:06:32,810 --> 00:06:35,710
Lustig left behind a list of rules.

95
00:06:35,810 --> 00:06:44,197
"Be a patient listener." "Never look bored." "Never boast, let your importance be quietly obvious."

96
00:06:44,297 --> 00:06:47,165
But the real lesson isn't on his list.

97
00:06:47,265 --> 00:06:50,697
The Tell is this: the secrecy was the con.

98
00:06:50,797 --> 00:06:55,688
"Don't tell anyone" is the sentence that has been in every scam since.

99
00:06:55,788 --> 00:07:00,432
If the deal only works if nobody checks it, it isn't a deal.

100
00:07:00,532 --> 00:07:06,531
If you want to see how someone fooled the entire art world with forgeries, that's next.

101
00:07:06,631 --> 00:07:09,275
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
